---
/**
 * Race Control, for the person the rule is actually about.
 *
 * The traffic-light rule is printed on page 61 of the book, in the "For Parents & Guardians"
 * section, and /parents renders it for adults. Three separate reviews noticed the same gap:
 * there is nowhere on this site that explains the rule to a thirteen-year-old. They meet it
 * as a coloured label on a build card, after they have already opened the build — which is
 * to say, after the decision.
 *
 * So this page is the rule, first, in the reader's own language, before anything is at
 * stake. Deliberately static: no JavaScript, no widget, no questionnaire that stores an
 * answer. It is a thing to read, and a thing a family can print and put on a fridge.
 *
 * The book's own wording is quoted verbatim in `book`. Everything in `you` and in the
 * examples is site-authored, written by applying that printed rule — the same boundary the
 * nineteen derived build flags are under, and stated on the page rather than blurred.
 */
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import ChapterFurniture from "../components/ChapterFurniture.astro";
import Flag from "../components/Flag.astro";
import PageRef from "../components/PageRef.astro";
import { FAMILY_RULE } from "./reality-check/_checks";
import { FLAG_RULE_PAGE } from "../lib/build-meta";

const TIERS = [
  {
    level: "green" as const,
    heading: "Green — go",
    /** Verbatim, printed page 61. */
    book: "Green decisions are low-cost and reversible.",
    you: "Just do it. Nobody needs to approve this and nothing breaks if it goes wrong. Most of what is on this site is green.",
    like: [
      "Writing your idea down",
      "Making a first version nobody sees",
      "Practising a skill for twenty minutes",
      "Sorting what you spent last week",
      "Building a demo for a made-up client",
    ],
  },
  {
    level: "yellow" as const,
    heading: "Yellow — say it out loud first",
    book: "Yellow decisions involve public posting, small spending, or contact with new people and require a check-in.",
    you: "Tell an adult before, not after. This is a conversation, not permission — they are looking at it with you, and most of the time they will say go ahead.",
    like: [
      "Posting something publicly with your name on it",
      "Spending a small amount of your own money",
      "Messaging someone you have never met",
      "Putting your work somewhere strangers can see it",
      "Agreeing to do something for someone by a date",
    ],
  },
  {
    level: "red" as const,
    heading: "Red — an adult does it with you",
    book: "Red decisions involve contracts, financial products, travel, identity documents, large payments, or in-person meetings and require direct adult involvement.",
    you: "Not you on your own. An adult is in the room, on the account and on the signature. This is not because you cannot be trusted — it is because these are the ones that are hard to undo.",
    like: [
      "Anything you have to sign",
      "Anything where money moves",
      "Sending ID, a passport page or a bank card",
      "Meeting someone in person",
      "Travelling somewhere for it",
      "Anything to do with investing or crypto",
    ],
  },
];

/** Three questions, in order. The first yes decides it. */
const DECIDE = [
  {
    q: "Does it involve signing, money moving, ID, travelling, or meeting someone in person?",
    then: "red" as const,
    say: "Then it is red. Stop and get an adult in it before you do anything else — including replying.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does it involve posting publicly, spending a little, or talking to someone new?",
    then: "yellow" as const,
    say: "Then it is yellow. Say it out loud to an adult first. Two minutes, before it happens.",
  },
  {
    q: "Neither of those?",
    then: "green" as const,
    say: "Then it is green. Go and make it.",
  },
];
---

<Page
  title="Race Control"
  description="The traffic-light rule from Teen & Grow Rich, explained for the person it is about. Green: go. Yellow: tell an adult first. Red: an adult does it with you. Three questions, and the first yes decides."
  printedPage={FLAG_RULE_PAGE}
>
  <ChapterFurniture
    eyebrow="Race control"
    title="Green, yellow, red"
    subtitle="One rule for every decision in this book. Three questions, and the first yes decides it."
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        In racing, flags come from race control and every driver on the track obeys them —
        not because they are being told off, but because the person with the flag can see
        the whole circuit and the driver can only see the next corner. That is the whole
        idea. This is not about whether you are sensible. It is about who can see what.
      </p>
      <p class="meta"><PageRef page={FLAG_RULE_PAGE} kind="For Parents & Guardians" /></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* The decision, before the explanation. Someone who arrives here with a message open in
      another tab needs an answer in ten seconds, not a page about safety culture. */}
  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="decide">
    <h2 id="decide">Work out which one it is</h2>
    <ol class="decide">
      {
        DECIDE.map((d, i) => (
          <li class="decide__step">
            <p class="decide__n" aria-hidden="true">{i + 1}</p>
            <div class="decide__body">
              <h3 class="decide__q">{d.q}</h3>
              <p class="decide__flag"><Flag level={d.then} size="sm" /></p>
              <p class="decide__say">{d.say}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
    <p class="decide__note">
      Answer them in order and stop at the first yes. If you are not sure which one
      something is, treat it as the more careful colour — that costs you a conversation,
      and being wrong the other way costs a lot more.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="tiers">
    <h2 id="tiers">What each colour actually means</h2>

    <div class="tiers">
      {
        TIERS.map((t) => (
          <article class={`tier tier--${t.level}`}>
            <div class="tier__head">
              <Flag level={t.level} />
              <h3 class="tier__heading">{t.heading}</h3>
            </div>

            <p class="tier__you">{t.you}</p>

            <p class="tier__like-label">Things that are {t.level}</p>
            <ul class="tier__like">{t.like.map((l) => <li>{l}</li>)}</ul>

            <p class="tier__book">
              <span class="tier__book-label">The book's words</span>
              {t.book}
            </p>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <p class="derived">
      The quoted line under each colour is printed in the book, on page {FLAG_RULE_PAGE}.
      Everything above it is this site putting that rule into the words you would actually
      use — including the examples, which the book does not print. If one of them looks
      wrong to you, the printed rule wins.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="rule">
    <h2 id="rule">The one line worth remembering</h2>
    <blockquote class="rule">
      <p>{FAMILY_RULE}</p>
      <footer><PageRef page={FLAG_RULE_PAGE} kind="For Parents & Guardians" /></footer>
    </blockquote>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        That second sentence is the useful one. A real offer is still there tomorrow. If
        something cannot survive you sleeping on it and showing one adult, that is not bad
        luck about timing — that is the whole trick, and it is the most common one there is.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="adults">
    <h2 id="adults">“An adult” does not only mean a parent</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A teacher, a coach, a librarian, a youth worker, a school counsellor, an aunt or
        uncle, a brother or sister over 18. It has to be someone who will actually look at
        the thing with you — not the person who is easiest to get a yes from.
      </p>
      <p>
        If there is nobody at home you can ask, that is not a dead end and it does not mean
        you are on your own. <a href="/reality-check">Every Reality Check</a> names who to
        raise that particular thing with, and several of them assume the answer is not a
        parent.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        Grown-ups reading over a shoulder: <a href="/parents">Race Control for adults</a> has
        the same rule with the family agreement, what to ask, and what this site does and
        does not do with anything typed into it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-inline-size: 72rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: clamp(1.25rem, 5vw, 3rem);
  }

  .prose {
    max-inline-size: var(--container-measure);
  }

  .prose p + p {
    margin-block-start: 0.9em;
  }

  .lede {
    margin-block-start: 1.75rem;
    font-size: var(--text-subtitle);
    line-height: var(--text-subtitle--line-height);
    color: var(--text-strong);
  }

  .meta {
    margin-block-start: 0.75rem;
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .section {
    margin-block-start: 3rem;
  }

  .section > h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-h2);
    line-height: var(--text-h2--line-height);
    color: var(--text-section);
    max-inline-size: var(--container-measure);
  }

  /* --- The three questions ---------------------------------------------------- */

  .decide {
    max-inline-size: var(--container-measure);
    margin-block-start: 1.25rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--rule-hairline);
  }

  .decide__step {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2.25rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0.75rem;
    align-items: start;
    padding-block: 1rem;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--rule-hairline);
  }

  .decide__n {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .decide__q {
    font-size: var(--text-heading);
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--text-strong);
  }

  .decide__flag {
    margin-block-start: 0.5rem;
  }

  .decide__say {
    margin-block-start: 0.35rem;
  }

  .decide__note {
    max-inline-size: var(--container-measure);
    margin-block-start: 1rem;
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* --- The three colours ------------------------------------------------------ */

  .tiers {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-block-start: 1.5rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 56rem) {
    .tiers {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
      align-items: start;
    }
  }

  .tier {
    padding: 1rem 1.1rem 1.2rem;
    background: var(--surface-card);
    /* Edge weight AND colour, so the three stay distinguishable on a black-and-white
       photocopy — the way a family will actually put this on a fridge. */
    border-inline-start-style: solid;
  }

  .tier--green {
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--flag-green);
  }

  .tier--yellow {
    border-inline-start: 5px dashed var(--flag-yellow);
  }

  .tier--red {
    border-inline-start: 7px double var(--flag-red);
  }

  .tier__head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
  }

  .tier__heading {
    font-size: var(--text-heading);
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--text-strong);
  }

  .tier__you {
    margin-block-start: 0.6rem;
  }

  .tier__like-label {
    margin-block-start: 1rem;
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: var(--text-label--letter-spacing);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .tier__like {
    margin-block-start: 0.4rem;
    padding-inline-start: 1.1rem;
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .tier__book {
    margin-block-start: 1rem;
    padding-block-start: 0.8rem;
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--rule-hairline);
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .tier__book-label {
    display: block;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: var(--text-label--letter-spacing);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-strong);
  }

  .derived {
    max-inline-size: var(--container-measure);
    margin-block-start: 1.25rem;
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* --- The family rule -------------------------------------------------------- */

  .rule {
    max-inline-size: var(--container-measure);
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1rem;
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--accent-dot);
  }

  .rule p {
    font-size: var(--text-subtitle);
    line-height: var(--text-subtitle--line-height);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--text-strong);
  }

  .rule footer {
    margin-block-start: 0.5rem;
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .aside {
    margin-block-start: 1.25rem;
    padding-block-start: 1rem;
    border-block-start: 3px solid var(--accent-dot);
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  a {
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  /* A family printing this puts it somewhere visible. Three colours become three edge
     weights and three edge styles on paper, and the cards must not split across a page. */
  @media print {
    .tiers {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 0.5rem;
    }

    .tier {
      background: none;
      break-inside: avoid;
    }

    .decide__step {
      break-inside: avoid;
    }
  }
</style>
